  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe large low pressure system is currently performing as\nadvertised across the region. Rain continues to spread across\nthe area this afternoon with rainfall amounts currently in the\nhalf inch to one inch range for most locations. Northeast winds\nare starting to uptick with gusts in the 25 to 35 mph range and\nwill peak later this evening, mainly in Northwest Ohio and along\nthe Lake Erie shoreline. Areas of Northwest Ohio will have some\npotential for 50 mph wind gusts this evening and the Wind\nAdvisory remains in effect. The strong northeast flow will \nallow for water levels to rise in the western basin of Lake \nErie about one foot above minor flood stage, and some lowland \nflooding is possible this evening. A Lakeshore Flood Warning is\nin effect for these locations.\n\nThe low will approach the region tonight and there will be a\nslight lull in the rain activity before the system pulls away\nfrom the region and to the east. Rain will fill back in across\nthe area and winds will abruptly shift to the northwest. Rain\nwill taper off areawide through the day on Friday before\ntransitioning to a lake enhanced rain situation with continued\nrain showers expected over the eastern half of the area through\nSaturday morning. The temperatures on Saturday morning have\nmoderated enough where any snow potential is becoming extremely\nlimited.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA trough will deepen over the central United States on Sunday,\nsupporting the development of a low pressure system. This system\nwill move through the Ohio Valley on Sunday into Monday and\nbring more rain to the region. Overall, rain amounts won't be\ntoo much, but enough to be a dreary last Sunday of November. The\nbackside of this system still appears cold to allow for some\nlake enhanced rain showers to linger into Monday with some wet\nsnowflakes possible, mainly in the higher hills of NW PA.",
